"2009\"],[\"Chinstrap\",\"Dream\",\"50.2\",\"18.7\",\"198\",\"3775\",\"female\",\"2009\"]]},\"x\":null,\"z\":null},\"config\":{\"graphType\":\"Scatter2D\",\"isR\":true,\"xAxis\":[\"flipper_length_mm\"],\"yAxis\":[\"body_mass_g\"],\"colorBy\":\"species\",\"title\":\"Palmer penguins — flipper length vs body mass\"},\"events\":null,\"afterRender\":null},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nInside `plot_with_settings`, that interactivity sits in the usual card, with the resize and download\ncontrols you already use:\n\n![Three canvasXpress plots inside plot_with_settings — select an element, open the customizer, download as HTML.](penguins-demo.gif)\n\n## Describe the chart instead of configuring it\n\nBecause the render path is generic, you inherit whatever the widget can do. canvasXpress, for\ninstance, can build a chart from a plain-English prompt — no `graphType`, no axes:\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nplot_with_settings_srv(\n  \"p\",\n  plot_r = reactive(canvasXpress(\n    data   = pg,\n    prompt = \"Create a scatter plot of bill_depth_mm vs body_mass_g and color by species\"\n  ))\n)\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n## Try it live\n\nThere's a running demo — three canvasXpress plots inside `plot_with_settings` cards:\n\n[**canvasxpress.shinyapps.io/teal-canvasxpress-demo**](https://canvasxpress.shinyapps.io/teal-canvasxpress-demo/)\n\nor install the PR build and run it yourself:\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\npak::pak(\"pharmaverse/teal.widgets#371\")\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n## Why it matters\n\nInteractive dashboards usually mean leaving the tools you trust for a BI platform, and losing\nreproducibility in the trade. This keeps both: the module model and reproducible, code-defined\nfigures of teal, plus the interactivity and portability of an htmlwidget. The figure is still\ncode — it just moves now.\n\nThe discussion that started it is\n[teal#1757](https://github.com/pharmaverse/teal/discussions/1757); the PR is\n[teal.widgets#371](https://github.com/pharmaverse/teal.widgets/pull/371). Feedback from teal users\nis welcome, especially on how you'd want widget support wired into the module API.\n",
